<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<metadata>

  <!--
    The wireframe mapper hierarchy is generic - WireframeMapper<T : View, W : Wireframe> - and its
    single member erases to map(Object, MappingContext, AsyncJobStatusCallback, InternalLogger).
    The generator binds the interface against the erased signature but does not emit a matching
    member on the abstract bases, so BaseWireframeMapper fails to implement the interface it
    declares (CS0535) and BaseAsyncBackgroundWireframeMapper overrides a method that consequently
    does not exist (CS0115).

    The whole mapper package is dropped from the BINDING rather than patched. Three things make
    that the right trade:

      - remove-node affects generated C# only. Every one of these 28 types is still in the .aar and
        still runs, so Session Replay records exactly as much as it would otherwise - including
        through MaterialExtensionSupport and ComposeExtensionSupport, whose mappers are resolved by
        Kotlin, not by C#.
      - The only thing removing them costs is authoring a custom mapper in C#, which means
        subclassing a generic Kotlin base and returning Wireframe records. There is no realistic
        MAUI scenario for it.
      - Patching instead would mean hand-writing JNI marshalling for an erased generic member on
        two abstract bases, and repeating it for each of the six subclasses.

    ExtensionSupport survives: its members that reference these types are dropped with them, but
    the interface itself, MaterialExtensionSupport and ComposeExtensionSupport remain, which is
    what SessionReplayConfiguration.Builder.AddExtensionSupport needs.
  -->
  <remove-node path="/api/package[@name='com.datadog.android.sessionreplay.recorder.mapper']" />

</metadata>
